      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Redford was named after its original landowner, B., whose family helped lay the town’s foundations in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/redford_missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1880s saw a boom in logging as railroads reached the area, transporting oak, hickory, and pine to builders and fuel suppliers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/redford_missouri",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Sinkin Creek skirts the southwest side of Redford, a tributary that once powered local mills and supported early homesteads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redford%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
